on a small roof dumps over 600 gallons right by your walls."},{"role":"flood","text":"The ditch floods."},{"role":"stakes","text":"Five feet down, that water pushes over 300 pounds on each square foot, and the wall cracks."},{"role":"fix1","text":"So you fill the ditch with gravel, but the water still sits at the bottom."},{"role":"fix2","text":"So you lay a pipe full of holes under the gravel."},{"role":"name","text":"This is a French drain."},{"role":"how","text":"Water seeps in through the holes, and the pipe slopes downhill to carry it away."},{"role":"fix3","text":"Wrap the gravel in fabric so mud can't clog it."},{"role":"myth","text":"And the French in French drain is not France."},{"role":"mythFact","text":"It is Henry French, a New England farmer and judge who wrote the book on drains in 1859."},{"role":"finish","text":"Add the grass back on top."},{"role":"congrats","text":"Congratulations, you have built a French drain."}]}